Written in a second class of the same package, the statement `g.coins` (private) is refused: "coins has private access in Gullak"; `g.audit()` gives "audit() has private access in Gullak". The statements that use the default member notes, the protected member seals and the public member slots all compile and display `5`, `2` and `1`.

```java
class Gullak {
    private int coins = 40;
    int notes = 5;
    protected int seals = 2;
    public int slots = 1;
    private void audit() {
        System.out.println("audit done");
    }
    public int total() {
        return coins + notes * 10;
    }
}
```

A program is compiled as one file, so every class in it belongs to one package. The access of a subclass in another package is known only from the table.

**Private is class-wide:** code inside a class may read the private members of any object of that class, as `other.sales` in the method beats of Stall (prints `true`), but code outside the class may not.

An **accessor** (getter) returns the value of a private member. A **mutator** (setter) changes it, and can reject an invalid value.

```java
class Thermo {
    private double reading;
    public void setReading(double t) {
        if (t >= -10 && t <= 55)
            reading = t;
        else
            System.out.println("Rejected: " + t);
    }
    public double getReading() {
        return reading;
    }
}
```

With the readings 31.5 and 80 the program prints `Rejected: 80.0 / 31.5`: the second reading is refused and the stored value stays.

If the member were public (class Raw), any class could store 80 and the program would print `80.0`.

Private data with a getter and no setter is **read-only** for other classes: `b.setBadgeNo(9)` gives "cannot find symbol" and `b.badgeNo = 9` gives "badgeNo has private access in Badge".

A constructor may be private too: `new Mint()` from another class gives "Mint() has private access in Mint".

A top-level class may be public or have default access; private, protected and static before it give errors such as "modifier private not allowed here".

```java
class Piggy {
    private int rupees;
    public void deposit(int amt) {
        if (amt > 0)
            rupees = rupees + amt;
    }
    public int getRupees() {
        return rupees;
    }
}
```

The deposits 250, -40 and 100 leave `350` in the box: the negative amount is ignored.

The **scope** of a variable is the part of a program in which it can be used. The place of declaration and the keyword static decide the kind.

- **Instance variable:** in the class, outside the methods, no static; one copy for each object.
- **Static (class) variable:** in the class with static; one copy shared by all objects; reached as `TokenDesk.next`.
- **Argument (formal) variable:** in the header of a method or constructor; a copy of the value passed.
- **Local variable:** inside a method, constructor or block; known from its declaration to the end of the block.

```java
public class Cafe {
    static int served = 0;
    int price;
    Cafe(int p) {
        price = p;
    }
    int order(int qty) {
        int bill = qty * price;
        served = served + qty;
        return bill;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cafe c = new Cafe(15);
        int amount = c.order(4);
        System.out.println(amount + " " + served);
    }
}
```

The program prints `60 4`. Here served is static, price is an instance variable, p and qty are argument variables, and bill, c and amount are local variables.

After three TokenDesk objects the static variable next is `4`, and it can be read even before any object exists (`1`).

```java
class Tally {
    int inst = 0;
    static int stat = 0;
    int bump() {
        int local = 0;
        local++;
        inst++;
        stat++;
        return local;
    }
}

public class Run {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Tally a = new Tally();
        Tally b = new Tally();
        System.out.println(a.bump() + " " + a.bump() + " " + b.bump());
        System.out.println(a.inst + " " + b.inst + " " + Tally.stat);
    }
}
```

Output: `1 1 1 / 2 1 3`. The local variable restarts at every call (1 1 1), the instance variable counts per object (2 and 1) and the static variable counts all calls (3).

**Blocks:** a variable declared in a for header or in an if block cannot be used after the block (`cannot find symbol`), and a name that an outer block already uses cannot be declared again (`variable x is already defined in method main(String[])`).

**Hiding:** a parameter or local variable with the name of a data member hides it; the keyword **this** reaches the member: the unfixed method leaves the member at `10`, the version with this.level gives `25`.

**Static context:** the static method main cannot use an instance variable directly (`non-static variable count cannot be referenced from a static context`); it needs an object. **Lifetime:** local and argument variables live from the call to the return, instance variables with their object, static variables as long as the program. Data members get default values (`0 0.0 false`); local variables get none.

